---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SlimNav from "../components/SlimNav.astro";
import SubPageNav from "../components/SubPageNav.astro";
import { ARTIST_ID } from "../lib/siteIdentity";

const petitionUrl =
  "https://www.change.org/p/create-a-narrative-reset-process-for-continuing-tv-series";
---

<Layout
  title="The Right to a Narrative Reset — Q888"
  description="A proposal by Alexander Sestakovs / Q888 for giving continuing television stories permission to reconsider their direction without erasing what already exists."
  canonicalPath="/narrative-reset/"
  pageType="article"
  mainEntityType="CreativeWork"
  mainEntityName="The Right to a Narrative Reset"
  mainEntityData={{
    author: { "@id": ARTIST_ID },
    genre: "Essay",
    about: ["Television storytelling", "Narrative continuity", "Creative authorship"],
  }}
  keywords={["Narrative Reset", "television storytelling", "series continuity", "creative authorship", "Q888"]}
>
  <SubPageNav />
  <div class="hidden md:block"><SlimNav /></div>

  <main class="narrative-reset">
    <header class="narrative-hero" aria-labelledby="narrative-title">
      <div class="narrative-film" aria-hidden="true">
        <video
          data-narrative-film
          data-src="/videos/narrative-reset.mp4"
          muted
          loop
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          tabindex="-1"
        ></video>
      </div>
      <div class="narrative-hero-shade" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="narrative-hero-copy">
        <h1 id="narrative-title">The Right to a Narrative Reset</h1>
        <p class="narrative-deck">
          When a story loses its way, should it really be forbidden from finding
          another way forward?
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="narrative-essay" id="essay">
      <div class="narrative-route" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <p>
        I started thinking about Narrative Reset because of <em>The Gentlemen</em>,
        but the more I considered it, the less it felt like an idea about one
        television series. It became a question about the rules we quietly accept
        when we watch continuing stories.
      </p>
      <p>
        One rule seems so natural that we rarely notice it: once something has
        been released, it becomes history. Whatever happens in a season now
        belongs to the story, and everything that follows must carry it forward.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before release, the process works differently. Scenes are rewritten,
        endings replaced, characters removed or rescued, and months of work
        abandoned because somebody discovers a better direction. We accept this
        as part of making something creative.
      </p>
      <p>Then the season comes out, and the door closes.</p>
      <p>
        That is what began to trouble me. Not because every unpopular decision
        should be reversed, and certainly not because audiences should be able to
        vote writers out of their own work. I began wondering why changing your
        mind is normal until the moment a story is released—and almost forbidden
        afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        We revise old rules when the world around them changes. Technology
        changes, culture changes, and so does the way television remains available
        to us. A season no longer appears once and disappears into broadcast
        history. It can sit on a platform for years, beside everything that came
        before and everything that follows. Someone can discover an entire series
        a decade later and watch it in a week.
      </p>
      <p>So perhaps continuity can become more flexible too.</p>
      <p>That is where Narrative Reset begins.</p>
      <p>
        I do not imagine a giant reset button that wipes out a season. The
        original version remains. Nobody loses it. If you loved that version, it
        is still yours to watch and defend. The only assumption that changes is
        the idea that one existing continuation must remain the only continuation
        a fictional world is allowed to have.
      </p>
      <p>A Narrative Reset would leave a small door open.</p>
      <p>
        Perhaps the people making a series look back and realise that a particular
        direction closed more possibilities than it opened. Perhaps a character
        has travelled so far from what made them compelling that the writers
        become curious about another path. Or perhaps nothing is technically
        “wrong”; another version of the future simply begins to feel more alive.
      </p>
      <p>The usual answer is that the story has already moved on.</p>
      <p>My question is: why can’t it sometimes move sideways?</p>
      <p>
        That is what I mean by the <em>right</em> in <strong>The Right to a
        Narrative Reset</strong>. I do not mean a legal right, or a right
        belonging to fans to force a studio into a rewrite. It is closer to giving
        storytelling a new permission.
      </p>
      <p>
        Film and television have built strong conventions around canon and
        continuity, and those conventions exist for good reasons. I am not
        interested in destroying them. I am interested in adding one small escape
        route for the moments when following the old rule creates less freedom
        than breaking it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The decision would still belong to the creators and rights-holders. An
        audience can complain, argue, recognise patterns and draw attention to
        something important, but it should never become the author by majority
        vote. The point is not to take control away from creators. It is to give
        them one more option.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="narrative-thesis">
        <span>Narrative Reset is not a demand to change a story.</span>
        <strong>It is permission to change one.</strong>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        For me, <em>The Gentlemen</em> is where this stopped being an abstract
        thought.
      </p>
      <p>
        I loved the first season. It gave me a clear sense of who these people
        were, where their moral boundaries sat, how quickly the story moved and
        what kind of world I was watching. When the second season arrived, I felt
        enough distance from that benchmark to start questioning something larger
        than whether I personally liked it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other viewers may see no problem in the transition between the seasons.
        That does not weaken the idea. In fact, it clarifies it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am not trying to prove that my reading of <em>The Gentlemen</em> is
        correct and everyone else has missed something. I use it because it gave
        me the experience that produced the question. I watched a continuing
        story take a direction that felt very different to me and, instead of
        thinking only, “I wish they had done this differently,” I began wondering
        why the alternative now existed only in imagination.
      </p>
      <p>
        And imagination is not the problem. I can already think of ways the story
        might escape its current path—one of them completely ridiculous, involving
        Eddie waking from something like a narcotic trip and forcing us to
        reconsider what we thought we had watched. From there, I could invent an
        entire alternative season.
      </p>
      <p>But nobody needs that from me.</p>
      <p>That is the point.</p>
      <p>
        My particular solution is not important. Somebody else would imagine
        another, and the people actually making the series could find a direction
        neither of us would have considered. Narrative Reset interests me because
        it creates that space rather than filling it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The word <em>branch</em> began to feel useful. A branch does not destroy
        the rest of the tree. It simply grows somewhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        We already understand this in other parts of culture. The same novel can
        become several very different films. A play can survive hundreds of
        interpretations. Comic-book characters can exist in contradictory
        versions without the universe collapsing. Films receive different cuts.
        Stories are remade, reinterpreted and retold because fiction does not
        always have to obey the same kind of history as reality.
      </p>
      <p>
        Continuing television is simply more attached to the idea that the road
        behind it determines the road ahead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perhaps it should remain that way most of the time. I only want the
        possibility that sometimes it does not have to.
      </p>
      <p>
        If Narrative Reset ever became real, there would need to be a moment when
        someone asks whether another direction is worth considering. We can call
        that <strong>Narrative Reconsideration</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not picture a huge committee or an audience tribunal. It could be
        much simpler: a recognised point at which the people responsible for a
        continuing story can look at where it has arrived and decide whether they
        still want to follow the same road.
      </p>
      <p>Most of the time, they probably would.</p>
      <p>Sometimes, they might not.</p>
      <p>
        That is enough for me at this stage. I am not trying to write the final law
        of television. I am putting a possibility into the world and seeing
        whether other people recognise something useful in it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I suspect many people will arrive here with another series already in
        mind. You do not need to have watched <em>The Gentlemen</em> to know the
        feeling of loving a world and then slowly finding it harder to recognise.
        Sometimes that change makes a story better. Sometimes it does not.
        Narrative Reset does not need to decide which is true for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        It asks only whether a story should always lose the freedom to reconsider
        itself because one version has already been shown to us.
      </p>
      <p>
        The petition is my way of testing whether this idea makes sense beyond my
        own experience. I do not see a signature as a vote to rewrite <em>The
        Gentlemen</em>, or as support for my imaginary version of what might happen
        next.
      </p>
      <p>It is a vote for the possibility.</p>
      <p>A small permission for continuing stories to keep one door unlocked.</p>

      <footer class="narrative-ending">
        <a href={petitionUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <span>Sign</span>
          The Right to a Narrative Reset petition
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <p>— <strong>Alexander Sestakovs / Q888</strong></p>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>

  <script>
    type NarrativeWindow = Window & {
      __narrativeFilmAbort?: AbortController;
      __narrativeFilmObserver?: IntersectionObserver;
    };

    function initNarrativeFilm() {
      const video = document.querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>(
        "[data-narrative-film]",
      );
      if (!video) return;

      const pageWindow = window as NarrativeWindow;
      pageWindow.__narrativeFilmAbort?.abort();
      pageWindow.__narrativeFilmObserver?.disconnect();

      const controller = new AbortController();
      const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
      pageWindow.__narrativeFilmAbort = controller;

      // CHOICE: the gentler pace lets the film introduce the idea without hurrying the reader.
      video.defaultPlaybackRate = 0.8;
      video.playbackRate = 0.8;

      const unloadFilm = () => {
        video.pause();
        video.removeAttribute("src");
        video.load();
      };

      const syncMotionPreference = () => {
        if (reducedMotion.matches) {
          pageWindow.__narrativeFilmObserver?.disconnect();
          unloadFilm();
          return;
        }

        if (!video.getAttribute("src")) {
          const source = video.dataset.src;
          if (source) {
            video.src = source;
            video.load();
          }
        }

        const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
          ([entry]) => {
            if (entry?.isIntersecting) {
              video.playbackRate = 0.8;
              void video.play().catch(() => undefined);
            } else {
              video.pause();
            }
          },
          { threshold: 0.08 },
        );
        observer.observe(video);
        pageWindow.__narrativeFilmObserver = observer;
      };

      reducedMotion.addEventListener("change", syncMotionPreference, {
        signal: controller.signal,
      });
      document.addEventListener(
        "astro:before-swap",
        () => {
          controller.abort();
          pageWindow.__narrativeFilmObserver?.disconnect();
        },
        { once: true, signal: controller.signal },
      );
      syncMotionPreference();
    }

    initNarrativeFilm();
    document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", initNarrativeFilm);
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  :global(html) {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
    scroll-padding-top: 3rem;
  }

  :global(body:has(.narrative-reset)) {
    --bg-fog: #09080c;
    --text-ink: #f6f0e7;
    --text-muted: #b8aea3;
    --line-soft: rgba(224, 190, 117, 0.2);
    background: #09080c;
  }

  .narrative-reset {
    --night: #09080c;
    --paper: #eee8df;
    --muted: #aaa09a;
    --gold: #d8b568;
    --violet: #a986d9;
    min-height: 100vh;
    color: var(--paper);
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 72% 37%, rgba(89, 50, 112, 0.08), transparent 30rem),
      var(--night);
  }

  .narrative-hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: min(94svh, 68rem);
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    overflow: hidden;
    isolation: isolate;
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 72% 37%, rgba(202, 161, 82, 0.18), transparent 22rem),
      radial-gradient(circle at 62% 48%, rgba(123, 73, 151, 0.2), transparent 34rem),
      #08070a;
  }

  .narrative-film,
  .narrative-film video,
  .narrative-hero-shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .narrative-film {
    z-index: -3;
  }

  .narrative-film video {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 46%;
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  .narrative-hero-shade {
    z-index: -2;
    background:
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(5, 4, 7, 0.88) 0%, rgba(7, 5, 9, 0.52) 44%, rgba(6, 5, 8, 0.2) 72%),
      linear-gradient(0deg, #09080c 0%, rgba(9, 8, 12, 0.12) 38%, rgba(9, 8, 12, 0.12) 74%, rgba(9, 8, 12, 0.46) 100%);
  }

  .narrative-hero-copy {
    width: min(100%, 82rem);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(8rem, 17vh, 13rem) clamp(1.4rem, 6vw, 6rem) clamp(9rem, 19vh, 13rem);
  }

  .narrative-hero h1 {
    max-width: 11ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(3.15rem, 7.7vw, 8rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 0.93;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: #e2c06f;
    text-shadow:
      0 3px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.68),
      0 0 32px rgba(210, 169, 85, 0.13);
  }

  .narrative-deck {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    font-family: "Syne", "Space Grotesk", ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.8vw, 1.38rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(246, 240, 231, 0.83);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .narrative-essay {
    position: relative;
    width: min(calc(100% - 3rem), 57rem);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(5.5rem, 9vw, 8.5rem) 0 clamp(6rem, 10vw, 9rem);
    font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.12rem, 1.4vw, 1.28rem);
    line-height: 1.7;
    letter-spacing: 0.002em;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .narrative-essay > p {
    margin: 0 0 1.15em;
  }

  .narrative-essay > p:first-of-type::first-letter {
    float: left;
    margin: 0.07em 0.12em 0 0;
    color: var(--gold);
    font-size: 3.55em;
    line-height: 0.82;
  }

  .narrative-essay em {
    color: #f5e7cc;
  }

  .narrative-essay strong {
    font-weight: 650;
    color: #f8f1e7;
  }

  .narrative-route {
    position: absolute;
    top: 8rem;
    bottom: 9rem;
    left: -3.5rem;
    width: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      transparent,
      rgba(216, 181, 104, 0.48) 8%,
      rgba(169, 134, 217, 0.24) 52%,
      rgba(216, 181, 104, 0.15) 88%,
      transparent
    );
  }

  .narrative-route::before,
  .narrative-route::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    width: 3.25rem;
    height: 1px;
    transform-origin: left center;
  }

  .narrative-route::before {
    top: 36%;
    transform: rotate(24deg);
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(216, 181, 104, 0.52), transparent);
  }

  .narrative-route::after {
    top: 71%;
    transform: rotate(-20deg);
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(169, 134, 217, 0.46), transparent);
  }

  .narrative-thesis {
    position: relative;
    margin: clamp(3.5rem, 6vw, 5.5rem) clamp(-2rem, -3vw, -1rem);
    padding: 0 0 0 clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    border: 0;
    font-family: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 3.8vw, 3.3rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }

  .narrative-thesis::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.2em;
    bottom: 0.1em;
    width: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(var(--gold), var(--violet));
    box-shadow: 0 0 24px rgba(180, 125, 211, 0.28);
  }

  .narrative-thesis span,
  .narrative-thesis strong {
    display: block;
  }

  .narrative-thesis span {
    color: rgba(238, 232, 223, 0.72);
  }

  .narrative-thesis strong {
    margin-top: 0.16em;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #f4d894;
  }

  .narrative-ending {
    margin-top: clamp(3.5rem, 6vw, 5.5rem);
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(216, 181, 104, 0.3);
  }

  .narrative-ending a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.15em 0.45em;
    color: #f1d797;
    font-family: "Syne", "Space Grotesk", ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.08rem, 2vw, 1.35rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(216, 181, 104, 0.45);
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.32em;
    transition: color 180ms ease, text-decoration-color 180ms ease;
  }

  .narrative-ending a span:first-child {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .narrative-ending a:hover,
  .narrative-ending a:focus-visible {
    color: #fff0c4;
    text-decoration-color: #e3c271;
  }

  .narrative-ending p {
    margin: 2.4rem 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .narrative-hero {
      min-height: 88svh;
    }

    .narrative-film video {
      object-position: 58% center;
    }

    .narrative-hero-shade {
      background:
        linear-gradient(0deg, #09080c 0%, rgba(9, 8, 12, 0.28) 46%, rgba(9, 8, 12, 0.42) 100%),
        linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(6, 5, 8, 0.66), rgba(6, 5, 8, 0.12));
    }

    .narrative-hero-copy {
      padding-bottom: 9.5rem;
    }

    .narrative-essay {
      width: min(calc(100% - 2.5rem), 43rem);
      padding-top: 4.8rem;
      padding-bottom: 6.5rem;
      line-height: 1.68;
    }

    .narrative-route {
      display: none;
    }

    .narrative-thesis {
      margin-right: 0;
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(html) {
      scroll-behavior: auto;
    }

    .narrative-ending a {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
